     "debunk_text": "## Are vaccine passports already in use?\n\nBefore the vaccine rollout, negative COVID-19 tests were required to travel to many countries. Some nations ordered travelers to quarantine in addition to presenting a negative test. This is largely still the case, even for those who are fully vaccinated.\n\nConcert attendees at Bloomfield Stadium in Tel Aviv, Israel, prepare to present their digital Green Passes, which certify they have been vaccinated against COVID-19. [...] As COVID-19 vaccinations pick up in some countries, debate is growing over the use of “vaccine passports”—digital or paper documentation that verifies an individual has been immunized against the new coronavirus. Officials are considering these vaccination certificates not only for international travel, but also for conferences, concerts, and sporting events, and even for day-to-day activities, including going to gyms, restaurants, work, and school. But their use could complicate vaccination [...] Concert attendees prepare to present their Green Passes, which certify they have been vaccinated against COVID-19, at Bloomfield Stadium in Tel Aviv, Israel. Jack Guez/AFP/Getty Images",
