     "evidence_id": "WEB-1",
     "debunk_text": "During and after the COVID-19 pandemic, claims have circulated alleging that mRNA vaccine products contain excessive levels of residual DNA that may integrate into the human genome. Among these claims, reports by McKernan and colleagues have been widely disseminated on preprint servers and social media platforms . These reports assert that residual DNA levels exceed regulatory limits and raise concerns about potential genomic integration. However, when such conclusions are based on [...] Since the report by McKernan et al. in 2023 , concerns have been raised that mRNA vaccines are “contaminated” with large amounts of DNA sufficient to cause health damage. During the production process of mRNA vaccines, fragments of the template DNA remain in the final mRNA product as residual DNA. McKernan and subsequent researchers have claimed that many mRNA vaccines contain massive amounts of DNA—hundreds of times the regulatory limit , , . If residual DNA were confirmed to [...] Residual DNA impurities in mRNA vaccine products serve as a quality parameter considered within longstanding international frameworks. Claims of excessive residual DNA and its genomic integration should be evaluated using validated analytical approaches and interpreted within established regulatory and biological contexts. Experimental findings, together with established regulatory science, do not support the assertion that residual DNA in approved mRNA vaccine formulations is present at levels",
