     "debunk_text": "Under the FSMA Current Good Manufacturing Practice, Hazard Analysis, and Risk-Based Preventive Controls for Human Food rule manufacturers and processors are responsible for considering risks from chemical hazards. Manufacturers of baby food are expected to have a food safety plan in place that includes a hazard analysis to identify any hazards and requiring a preventive control. If there are such hazards, the manufacturers would be required to have a preventive control, such as a plan to check [...] Through the Closer to Zero initiative, the FDA is working with the food industry to make baby food even safer than they already are by further reducing low levels of heavy metals. This page describes the FDA’s ongoing process to progressively drive down levels of heavy metals to ensure food intended for babies and young children is as safe as it can be.\n\n## The Closer to Zero Process\n\nTo meet the goals of Closer to Zero, the FDA focuses on:\n\n### Research and Analysis:\n\n### Regulation: [...] \\\\ Mercury. Our Closer to Zero initiative covers all heavy metals, but our research under Closer to Zero shows that mercury is not a concern in processed foods for babies and young children. The FDA has collected mercury data through its Toxic Elements compliance program (FY2009-2024, 195 samples) and two surveys of processed foods intended for babies and young children (FY2020-2022, 414 samples) and (FY2023-2024, 386 samples). Of these 995 samples collected, only 4 samples had total mercury",
