     "evidence_id": "WEB-3",
     "debunk_text": "The continuous antigenic drift of seasonal influenza viruses and frequent point mutations in the viral genome require reformulation of vaccines every 1 to 2 years to provide protection against circulating drifted strains. Overall, influenza vaccine effectiveness has varied from year to year and ranges from 40% to 60%.21 This demonstrates the importance of improving current influenza vaccines and developing universal influenza vaccines that enhance cross-protection. A great deal of effort is [...] against the virus, most recently incorporating A/H1N1, A/H3N2, B/Yamagata-like, and B/Victoria-like strains in the quadrivalent vaccine. Recommendations for what strains to include in the vaccine are made annually by the World Health Organization for both Northern and Southern hemispheres. In the 2020–2021 influenza season, an estimated 190 million doses of influenza vaccine were distributed in the United States, in part based on fears of a “twindemic” with severe acute respiratory syndrome [...] To achieve higher rates of efficacy, while maintaining safety, significant research in the development of so-called universal and other novel influenza vaccine platforms is occurring, with the idea of minimizing or eliminating the need for annual viral strain changes in vaccine composition, and the associated varying efficacy. The purpose of this brief review was to examine the types and limitations of currently available influenza vaccines and review the current state of the development of",
