     "evidence_id": "WEB-1",
     "debunk_text": "##### Quick Take\n\nA video circulating on social media falsely claims that vaccines for COVID-19 have a microchip that “tracks the location of the patient.” The chip, which is not currently in use, would be attached to the end of a plastic vial and provide information only about the vaccine dose. It cannot track people.\n\n##### Full Story\n\nCOVID-19 vaccines have begun rolling out, but so has misinformation about them. [...] “What that chip does is it has the unique serial number for each dose,” Walker said in the original interview. “It is designed so that there is no counterfeiting. It is designed so that we’ll know exactly that the right dose hasn’t expired. However, that chip only refers to the dose — there’s no personal information, no patient information. It’s simply like a barcode, only we know instantaneously where and when that dose has been used.” [...] A video claiming that the vials containing the vaccines have a microchip that “tracks the location of the patient” has been viewed more than 225,000 times on Facebook, according to data from CrowdTangle.\n\nThat’s false.\n\nThe claim is based on a misrepresentation of an interview with Jay Walker, an executive for a medical device company called ApiJect.",
     "evidence_source": "factcheck.org — https://www.factcheck.org/2020/12/covid-19-vaccines-dont-have-patient-tracking-devices",
     "evidence_url": "https://www.factcheck.org/2020/12/covid-19-vaccines-dont-have-patient-tracking-devices",
     "similarity": null,
