     "evidence_id": "WEB-2",
     "debunk_text": "and presence of comorbidities were collected. Descriptive statistics (mean, standard deviation) were performed by using SPSS. Among 2534 participants who were vaccinated, systemic side effects were reported by 14.4% (379/2534) after the first dose and 10.4% (265/2534) after the second dose across all vaccines. Among vaccine-specific groups, systemic side effects were observed in 6.6% (109/1635) and 5.2% (85/1635) for the first and second doses of Pfizer; 34.3% (136/396) and 42% (166/396) for [...] A study from the US corroborates these findings, showing that mRNA vaccines (Pfizer and Moderna) tend to elicit more systemic side effects compared to viral vector vaccines like AstraZeneca25, 181–192 (2021).\"). The relatively low rates of systemic side effects in our study (e.g., Pfizer: 1.8% for headache and 1.5% for fatigue) contrast with clinical trial data, where systemic effects occurred in up to 34–47% of participants29, 939–949 (2021).\"). [...] reported of all vaccines among all respondents. A small proportion of the respondents who took the Pfizer vaccine suffered from injection site pain (3.3%), allergic reaction (0.4%), and redness (0.2%). Likewise, among those who received the Moderna vaccine, 27.5%, 6.3%, and 6.1% of participants suffered from injection site pain, swelling and tenderness respectively. Moreover, around 10.7% of respondents who took the Sinovac vaccine mentioned having injection site pain (Table 3).",
