    },
    {
     "window": 9,
     "claim_text": "What you can see on the outside of this virus are these spikes.",
     "verdict": "NOT_FAKE_NEWS_WEB",
     "addresses": "yes",
     "stance": "asserting",
     "flagged_phrase": "",
     "reason": "The spike protein (S), present on the outer surface of the virion, is a crucial component for its attachment and entry to the host cells.",
     "evidence_id": "WEB-1",
     "debunk_text": "The virus has four important structural proteins, the envelope protein (E), the membrane protein (M), the spike protein (S), and the nucleocapsid protein (N). These proteins are important to regulate the virus structure and function. The spike protein (S), present on the outer surface of the virion, is a crucial component for its attachment and entry to the host cells. The virus entry requires the priming of the S protein by cellular proteases, which cleave it into a S1/S2 sites, with the [...] The virus spike protein is defined as a glycoprotein on the surface of a virus, such as SARS-CoV-2, that facilitates viral entry into host cells by binding to specific receptors, like the angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (ACE2) on human cells. It consists of two subunits, S1, which determines host range and cell adhesion, and S2, which aids in membrane fusion.\n\nAI generated definition based on:Advanced Methods in Biomedical Signal Processing and Analysis, 2023\n\nHow useful is this definition? [...] When the SARS-CoV-2 enters the airway, the virus spike protein (S) recognizes and binds the angiotensin-converting enzyme-2 receptor (ACE-2) on the surface of airway epithelial cells (AECs) . The receptor-bound spike protein is cleaved by the transmembrane protease serine 2 (TMPRSS2) followed by membrane-fusion or endosomal entry into the AECs . After entering the host cell, the virion releases viral ssRNA into the cytoplasm. The viral ssRNA replicates in the cytoplasm via RNA-dependent RNA",
     "evidence_source": "sciencedirect.com — https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/virus-spike-protein",
     "evidence_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/virus-spike-protein",
