     "reason": "A lack of control group means there is nothing to compare the results with, so you cannot tell whether a change was caused by the treatment.",
     "evidence_id": "WEB-1",
     "debunk_text": "A lack of control group means there is nothing to compare the results with, so you cannot tell whether a change was caused by the treatment or by the placebo effect, the natural course of the disease, regression to the mean or chance. Single-arm studies without a control group can generate hypotheses, but they rarely prove that a treatment works.\n\n### Should treatment and control groups be the same size? [...] Ask which group does not receive the intervention under investigation. That group is the control group; the group that receives the new treatment is the experimental group. In a clinical trial protocol, the control arm is named explicitly, for example as the placebo arm, the active comparator arm or the standard of care arm.\n\n### What does a lack of control group mean? [...] Can observational studies have control groups? Yes, but the researcher does not assign the treatment, so the meaning shifts. In an observational study:\n\n Cohort studies compare people exposed to a factor (for example, a medication or smoking) with an unexposed comparison group, and follow both for outcomes.\n Case-control studies start from the outcome: “cases” have the disease, “controls” are similar people without it. Here, controls are defined by the outcome, not by treatment.",
     "evidence_source": "viares.com — https://viares.com/blog/clinical-research-explained/control-group",
