     "debunk_text": "This reflects the judgement that if used non-fictionally, that is, to make an assertion, (1) is neither true nor false. Someone who asserts (1) as a claim about the actual world is not making a false claim, but is exhibiting confusion. Unless one is confused about the existence of Anna Karenina, one would not utter (1) as an assertion about how things actually are. (This contrasts with asserting (1) as a statement about the fiction Anna Karenina. I give an account of such uses in 4.2.) [...] The claim that, when used metafictionally, (1) has the same content as (51) can be understood in different ways. One can understand it to mean that, when used in this way, the LF of (1) includes the operator, that is, the operator is present at LF but is unpronounced.Footnote 28 Alternatively, one can hold that a metafictional use of (1) succeeds in expressing the content of (51), even though the operator is not present in the LF of (1).Footnote 29 I will not take a stand on this difference [...] There are many different labels for this use of fictional names.Footnote 26 I will call them “metafictional” uses. It is commonly agreed that metafictional uses are assertions—that is, when not used ironically, or in some other non-standard way. When one uses (1) to make a claim about Anna Karenina, one typically asserts something about Anna Karenina. Correspondingly, qua assertions, metafictional uses have truth-values at the actual world. As Recanati (2018. Fictional, metafictional,",
