    {
     "window": 2,
     "claim_text": "VAERS was created by Congress in 1990 to help medical experts track any sort of issue that may come up with a vaccine.",
     "verdict": "NOT_FAKE_NEWS_WEB",
     "addresses": "yes",
     "stance": "asserting",
     "flagged_phrase": "",
     "reason": "",
     "evidence_id": "WEB-1",
     "debunk_text": "Established in 1990, the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS) is a national early warning system to detect possible safety problems in U.S.-licensed vaccines. VAERS is co-managed by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) and the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). VAERS accepts and analyzes reports of adverse events (possible side effects) after a person has received a vaccination. Anyone can report an adverse event to VAERS. Healthcare professionals are required to",
     "evidence_source": "vaers.hhs.gov — https://vaers.hhs.gov/about.html",
     "evidence_url": "https://vaers.hhs.gov/about.html",
     "similarity": null,
