     "debunk_text": "• mRNA vaccines do not use the live virus that causes COVID-19.\nThey do not affect or interact with our DNA in any way.\n• mRNA never enters the nucleus of the cell, which is where our DNA (genetic material) is kept.\n• The cell breaks down the mRNA soon after it is finished using the mRNA’s instructions to make the viral protein that will trigger the body to make antibodies against the virus.\nLearn more about mRNA vaccines from CDC.\nWhat are the side effects of these vaccines? [...] No. The vaccines being used to prevent COVID-19 in the United States can’t make someone sick with the disease. None of the vaccines contain whole or live SARS-CoV-2, the virus that causes COVID-19. Getting vaccinated helps protect you by creating an antibody (immune system) response without getting you sick. People who join ongoing vaccine trials may be exposed to SARS-CoV-2 in their everyday lives, but not as a part of vaccine research. How do these vaccines work?",
