   "results": [
    {
     "window": 1,
     "claim_text": "In May, a team of engineers at Columbia announced a new implantable medical microchip, so tiny it can be injected into a human body with a syringe.",
     "verdict": "NOT_FAKE_NEWS_WEB",
     "addresses": "yes",
     "stance": "asserting",
     "flagged_phrase": "",
     "reason": "",
     "evidence_id": "WEB-1",
     "debunk_text": "In a May 12 press release, Columbia announced researchers had developed a medical microchip. At 0.1 cubic millimeters, the researchers report the device is the smallest single-chip system of its kind and can be injected with a hypodermic needle.\n\nBut just because the chip can fit inside a needle does not mean it is being used to secretly microchip people. [...] Now there's a twist. Columbia University announced May 12 that researchers had developed a tiny microchip capable of measuring vital signs — and small enough to be injected.\n\nThe online reaction was predictable.\n\n“Magnified many times. Tip of a hypodermic needle. Look what fits just inside…” a Facebook user captioned an image of the chip alongside a needle in a May 18 post. [...] We rate the claim that a microchip could fit inside a hypodermic needle MISSING CONTEXT, because its online presentation is misleading. Columbia Engineering is developing an injectable microchip that can internally track vitals. A spokesperson for the school confirmed it has yet to be tested on people and has nothing to do with vaccines. The COVID-19 vaccine is not being used to secretly microchip recipients.\n\n## Our fact-check sources:",
     "evidence_source": "usatoday.com — https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2021/05/26/fact-check-missing-context-posts-new-microchip-development/5232378001",
     "evidence_url": "https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2021/05/26/fact-check-missing-context-posts-new-microchip-development/5232378001",
     "similarity": null,
     "retrieved_ids": [
