     "evidence_id": "WEB-1",
     "debunk_text": "Online rumors are defined as unverified claims or stories that circulate widely before authoritative verification becomes available, leaving their factual status temporarily unresolved . This pre-verification stage creates a distinct decision environment in which veracity cannot be readily confirmed. Users therefore form judgments about truthfulness in the face of epistemic uncertainty rather than in the face of factual clarity , . In such settings, standard verification mechanisms are often [...] One common pattern on social media is the rapid spread of unverified posts following sudden and potentially risk-relevant events (e.g., food safety scares, industrial accidents, or disease outbreaks). In the early hours, many posts appear as urgent alerts. Their wording often highlights potential loss and danger, and they display strong social signals (e.g., rapid reposting and dense comment threads). Under such conditions, users often cannot verify factual accuracy, yet they still form [...] corroborated. Theoretically, the research clarifies how affect enters truthfulness evaluation in pre-verification digital environments where external evidentiary anchors are limited at the time of exposure. Practically, the results inform Emotion- and Social-Aware Decision-Support Systems (ES-DSS) that can help users calibrate emotional and social cues against evidentiary quality when evaluating unverified content.",
     "evidence_source": "sciencedirect.com — https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167923626000710",
     "evidence_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167923626000710",
