     "debunk_text": "many of the adverse reaction reports were misattributed every-day symptom experiences. In fact, a recent study has shown that >70% of side effects from COVID-19 vaccines are due to the nocebo effect . [...] In conclusion, media coverage of side effects from the Pfizer COVID-19 vaccine was associated with an increase in the reporting rate of the specific symptoms mentioned in the news items. Reports of myocarditis also exceeded the expected rate following the media coverage. This is likely due to the nocebo effect, with the media influencing expectations of side effects and prompting people to misattribute normal or anxiety-induced symptoms to the vaccine. The findings from this study suggest that [...] produces a number of symptoms, such as palpitations, rapid breathing and chest pain, which are then available to be misattributed to the vaccine in line with expectations . Anxiety has previously been shown to be associated with greater side effect reporting from vaccines [36,41]. However, in the current study anxiety was not a predictor for the reporting of myocarditis or pericarditis. A recent study has shown that COVID-19 related anxiety is associated with increased reporting of various",
