     "stance": "asserting",
     "flagged_phrase": "",
     "reason": "",
     "evidence_id": "WEB-1",
     "debunk_text": "As he has done for years, President Donald Trump claims – without evidence – that the federal Right to Try law he signed in 2018 has “saved thousands of lives.” But the White House provided no support for Trump’s claim about the law, which provides an alternative route for seriously ill patients to access unapproved drugs outside of clinical trials.\n\nResearchers who have long studied access to investigational drugs say the president is greatly exaggerating. [...] As we mentioned, there has been an increase in expanded access requests and approvals, so there is data to support the theory that the Right to Try law has had an indirect benefit. But there is no evidence to support the president’s claim that the law has “saved thousands of lives.” [...] We asked the White House for the number of patients treated under the Right to Try law and evidence that thousands of lives were saved. But it provided no support for Trump’s claim — which he repeated throughout the 2024 presidential campaign, including in his acceptance speech at the 2024 Republican convention. Most recently, the president said in April that Right to Try has “saved thousands and thousands of lives,” and he said it again in May, when he claimed the law has “saved thousands of",
     "evidence_source": "factcheck.org — https://www.factcheck.org/2026/06/no-evidence-for-trumps-right-to-try-claim",
     "evidence_url": "https://www.factcheck.org/2026/06/no-evidence-for-trumps-right-to-try-claim",
     "similarity": null,
