    {
     "window": 2,
     "claim_text": "The Defense Department says it's to acquire syringes for COVID vaccines.",
     "verdict": "NOT_FAKE_NEWS_WEB",
     "addresses": "yes",
     "stance": "asserting",
     "flagged_phrase": "",
     "reason": "The DOD press release states it awarded a $138 million contract for prefilled syringes for a future COVID‑19 vaccine.",
     "evidence_id": "WEB-3",
     "debunk_text": "##### Sources\n\nJohnson, Lorie. “RFID Chip May Be Tied to the New Coronavirus Vaccine.” CBN News. 22 May 2020.\n\nU.S. Department of Defense. Press release. “DOD Awards $138 Million Contract Enabling Prefilled Syringes for Future COVID-19 Vaccine.” 12 May 2020.\n\nApiJect. “When COVID-19 vaccine and injectable therapeutics become available, the RAPID Consortium can ease a U.S. supply chain problem by filling and finishing 300+ million prefilled syringes per month.” Accessed 14 Dec 2020.",
     "evidence_source": "factcheck.org — https://www.factcheck.org/2020/12/covid-19-vaccines-dont-have-patient-tracking-devices",
     "evidence_url": "https://www.factcheck.org/2020/12/covid-19-vaccines-dont-have-patient-tracking-devices",
     "similarity": null,
     "retrieved_ids": [
      "WEB-3",
      "WEB-1",
      "WEB-2"
     ],
     "evidence_origin": "web",
     "retrieved_at": "2026-11-11T01:35:55Z",
     "web_decision": "corpus did not address the claim; query=\"defense department syringes covid vaccines acquisition\""
    },
    {
     "window": 3,
     "claim_text": "claims online are questioning why the syringes have trackers in them.",
     "verdict": "NOT_FAKE_NEWS_WEB",
     "addresses": "yes",
     "stance": "asserting",
     "flagged_phrase": "",
     "reason": "FactCheck.org notes that many social‑media users posted false claims that COVID‑19 vaccine syringes contain tracking devices.",
     "evidence_id": "WEB-1",
     "debunk_text": "The ApiJect syringes work like an eyedropper, Walker explained, with a plastic pouch as the vial. The syringes can also be made with an optional chip that could be attached to the end of the plastic vial to store information about its contents. The chip is an RFID tag, which is short for radio frequency identification, and requires a device to scan and read the data. [...] “Neither Moderna or Pfizer are using our syringe at this point,” Hofman said.\n\nDespite the fact that the syringes aren’t currently being used for COVID-19 vaccines and the optional chips they use can’t track individuals, many Facebook users left comments on the video expressing a misunderstanding.\n\n“Nope, never had flu shot, won’t take this either! Especially one with a tracking device. What a crock,” wrote one user. [...] In reality, the chip would allow those administering the vaccine to check that the dose hasn’t expired and isn’t counterfeit, Hofman said. “It’s incapable of collecting any personal information,” he said.\n\nAlso, it’s worth noting that ApiJect’s syringes aren’t being used for either the currently available vaccine developed by Pfizer and the German biotech company BioNTech or the next one up for emergency use authorization, which was developed by Moderna.",
     "evidence_source": "factcheck.org — https://www.factcheck.org/2020/12/covid-19-vaccines-dont-have-patient-tracking-devices",
     "evidence_url": "https://www.factcheck.org/2020/12/covid-19-vaccines-dont-have-patient-tracking-devices",
     "similarity": null,
     "retrieved_ids": [
      "WEB-1",
      "WEB-2",
      "WEB-3"
     ],
     "evidence_origin": "web",
     "retrieved_at": "2026-11-11T01:35:58Z",
     "web_decision": "corpus did not address the claim; query=\"vaccine syringes contain tracking devices\""
    },
    {
     "window": 4,
     "claim_text": "Both talk about a government contract to buy syringes with RFID tracking for COVID vaccines.",
     "verdict": "NOT_FAKE_NEWS_WEB",
     "addresses": "yes",
     "stance": "asserting",
     "flagged_phrase": "",
     "reason": "The source explains the contract includes syringes with optional RFID tags that could indicate expiration but are not used to track individuals.",
     "evidence_id": "WEB-2",
     "debunk_text": "##### Sources\n\nJohnson, Lorie. “RFID Chip May Be Tied to the New Coronavirus Vaccine.” CBN News. 22 May 2020.\n\nU.S. Department of Defense. Press release. “DOD Awards $138 Million Contract Enabling Prefilled Syringes for Future COVID-19 Vaccine.” 12 May 2020.\n\nApiJect. “When COVID-19 vaccine and injectable therapeutics become available, the RAPID Consortium can ease a U.S. supply chain problem by filling and finishing 300+ million prefilled syringes per month.” Accessed 14 Dec 2020. [...] The ApiJect syringes work like an eyedropper, Walker explained, with a plastic pouch as the vial. The syringes can also be made with an optional chip that could be attached to the end of the plastic vial to store information about its contents. The chip is an RFID tag, which is short for radio frequency identification, and requires a device to scan and read the data. [...] That portion of the interview wasn’t included in the video that’s currently circulating. Instead, the video includes the falsehood that the chips would be used to track the location of individual patients.\n\nIt’s unclear how that would even work, though, since the RFID tag is attached to the vial and can’t be injected into a patient. Steven Hofman, spokesman for ApiJect, told us in an interview that the idea of the RFID tags being used to track individuals is “beyond fantasy.”",
     "evidence_source": "factcheck.org — https://www.factcheck.org/2020/12/covid-19-vaccines-dont-have-patient-tracking-devices",
     "evidence_url": "https://www.factcheck.org/2020/12/covid-19-vaccines-dont-have-patient-tracking-devices",
     "similarity": null,
     "retrieved_ids": [
      "WEB-1",
      "WEB-2",
      "WEB-3"
     ],
     "evidence_origin": "web",
     "retrieved_at": "2026-11-11T01:36:02Z",
     "web_decision": "corpus did not address the claim; query=\"government contract syringes RFID tracking COVID vaccines\""
